<article class="article-page">
<p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/guides/">Guides</a> / microSD card not detected</p>
<p class="page-kicker">Storage troubleshooting</p>
<h1 class="page-title">microSD card not detected: separate the reader, host, disk state, and card.</h1>
<p class="page-lead">“Not detected” can mean several different things: the reader never sees the card electrically, Windows sees a disk but no usable volume, File Explorer lacks a drive letter, or the card’s file system is damaged. The safest path is to identify which layer still works before writing anything to the card.</p>

<div class="article-summary"><strong>The five-minute answer</strong><p>Try a second known-compatible reader or host. On Windows, open <code>diskmgmt.msc</code> and run the read-only <code>Get-Disk</code> command. If the card appears as a physical disk, inspect its partition/volume state before changing it. If the card contains important data and appears RAW, Unallocated, or otherwise damaged, stop before formatting. If the data is backed up or disposable, the SD Association recommends its current SD Memory Card Formatter for SD-family media.</p></div>

<div class="article-body">
<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 01</p><h2>Rule out the reader and adapter first.</h2>
<p>Use another reader or another host that explicitly supports the card type and capacity. SD, SDHC, SDXC, and SDUC support depends on the host/reader. The SD Association notes that some older readers do not support newer capacity classes.</p>
<p>If you are using a microSD-to-SD adapter, test another adapter too. The adapter is a passive mechanical/electrical path that can fail independently of the card.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 02</p><h2>Ask Windows whether it sees a physical disk.</h2>
<p>Open Disk Management or run:</p>
<pre><code>Get-Disk</code></pre>
<p><code>Get-Disk</code> is a read-only Storage-module query and does not require you to initialize or format the media. If the card appears there but not in File Explorer, the problem is now partition/volume/drive-letter related rather than basic reader detection.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 03</p><h2>Treat Healthy, RAW, Unallocated, and missing as different states.</h2>
<div class="article-cards">
<div><strong>Healthy volume, no drive letter</strong><p>Assigning an unused drive letter can make the existing volume visible without formatting it.</p></div>
<div><strong>RAW or unexpected Unallocated</strong><p>If the card previously held data, stop before formatting or creating a new volume. Consider recovery first.</p></div>
<div><strong>No disk in multiple readers</strong><p>Reader compatibility is now less likely; card hardware failure becomes more plausible.</p></div>
</div></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 04</p><h2>Format only after the data decision.</h2>
<p>The SD Association warns that formatting removes recorded information and recommends its SD Memory Card Formatter for preparing SD/SDHC/SDXC/SDUC media. That is appropriate when the data is backed up, disposable, or the card is intentionally being repurposed—not as the first diagnostic action on the only copy of important files.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Decision</p><h2>Do you need another card?</h2>
<div class="article-cards">
<div><strong>No</strong><p>If another reader, drive-letter change, or supported reformat fixes a disposable card, the card may still be usable.</p></div>
<div><strong>Maybe</strong><p>If the card repeatedly drops offline or becomes unreadable across compatible devices, retire it from important storage even if it temporarily recovers.</p></div>
<div><strong>Recovery first</strong><p>If it contains irreplaceable files and now appears RAW/Unallocated or intermittently, minimize writes and prioritize data recovery over repair attempts.</p></div>
</div></section>

